<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huns</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hunnic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Huns were a nomadic people who appeared on the edges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the late fourth century CE and built a powerful empire, most famously under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>. Some historians have proposed that they were linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, though the connection remains debated. The question forms part of the discussion of the Xiongnu Empire's legacy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huns.jpg" alt="Huns" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Johann Nepomuk Geiger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hunnen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Huns left no sources of their own, and their origins and links to other steppe peoples remain uncertain. They probably came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. Roman writers equated them with earlier steppe peoples, calling them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massagetae">Massagetae</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a>. In the 18th century Joseph de Guignes proposed that they were descended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>. Scholars still dispute the connection, though recent archaeogenetic studies show some Hun-era individuals with DNA similar to ancient Mongolian populations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> became aware of the Huns in 376, when their invasion of the Pontic steppes drove thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> to seek refuge on the Lower Danube. In 395 they began their first large-scale attack on the Eastern Roman Empire, striking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, Cappadocia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. From 434 the brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bleda">Bleda</a> ruled together, and in 435 they forced the Eastern Roman Empire to sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-margus">Treaty of Margus</a>, which gave the Huns trade rights and annual tribute. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a> gave in to their demands in the Peace of Anatolius in 443. Bleda died in 445 and Attila ruled alone.</p><p>In 451 the Huns invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and fought a combined army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> at the Battle of the Catalaunian Fields, and in 452 they invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>'s death in 453 his empire faced a struggle between its Germanic vassals and the Hunnic rulers. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nedao">Battle of Nedao</a>, around 454, the Gepid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardaric">Ardaric</a> led a Germanic coalition against Attila's son Ellak, and the Huns lost many of their vassals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Huns practised pastoral nomadism, and their main method of warfare was mounted archery. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> their economy depended heavily on plunder and tribute from the Roman provinces. Their government is debated: Peter Heather sees a loose confederation, and Hyun Jin Kim argues for a far more centralised system. They ruled many peoples, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a>, Sarmatians, Heruli, Rugii, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a> and Sciri, whose own kings often remained in place.</p><p>Very few archaeological remains are firmly Hunnic. Large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> cauldrons are attributed to them, and between 50 and 80 per cent of burials in the Carpathian basin in the Hun period show artificial cranial deformation. Almost nothing is known of their religion, though divination is attested. Medieval Hungarian chronicles claimed descent from them, a claim mainstream scholarship has dismissed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Huns” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 370 the <b>Huns</b> defeated the Alans near the Don, and many Alans joined the Huns or moved west with the Goths; Alan cavalry is reported among the Gothic forces at the Battle of Adrianople in 378.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Huns</b>, who reached the region in the 370s, appear to have overrun much of its territory and probably ended the monarchy, although the city of Bosporus, the former Panticapaeum, survived and came under Byzantine control in the reign of Justinian I.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The greater danger came from the north: the Alans, and later the <b>Huns</b> and other steppe peoples, crossed the Caucasus by the mountain passes and by the narrow Caspian coastal route at Derbent, and in 135 an Alan invasion struck Albania and Media.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Frankish federates fought the Vandals as they approached the Rhine in 406, and Frankish troops served under Aetius against Attila at the Battle of the Catalaunian Plains in 451, while other Franks backed the <b>Huns</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hephthalites belong to what the numismatist Robert Göbl called the Iranian <b>Huns</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Huns</b> were a nomadic people first reported east of the Volga River, in what was then Scythia; their origins are uncertain, probably in Central Asia, and classical sources say they appeared in Europe suddenly around 370.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
